# Framework application model

An `App<P>` is the assembled dependency graph and runtime for one protocol `P`. It owns configuration, directories, hooks, shutdown, and the selected protocol's dispatch surface. Most binaries declare that runtime through `app!`; use the builder when the surrounding process needs direct control.

This framework section owns the application model and the framework-wide facilities used by every transport. Axum owns HTTP and WebSocket server behavior, RPC owns its server, and generated connection code lives under clients.

## Choose a framework task

| Task | Guide |
|---|---|
| declare the host, control lifecycle, or compose protocol processes | <GuideRef slug="framework/application">Application</GuideRef> |
| construct components, select providers, or choose lifetimes | <GuideRef slug="framework/dependency-injection">Dependency injection</GuideRef> |
| merge configuration sources or reload typed bindings | <GuideRef slug="framework/configuration">Configuration</GuideRef> |
| choose Cargo features, compilation targets, or application directories | <GuideRef slug="framework/platforms">Platforms</GuideRef> |
| package behavior as a plugin or author an application protocol | <GuideRef slug="framework/extensions">Extensions</GuideRef> |

All framework tasks assume a working application crate and the facade import shown in <GuideRef slug="getting-started">getting started</GuideRef>. Choose server and client features before relying on a protocol-specific prelude.

## Assemble an application

Start with the facade prelude, the protocol type, and the values your application registers. The
`App` alias in the protocol prelude keeps the application type tied to that protocol without a
manual generic parameter.

<Example title="Build an application directly">

```rust
use upwell::axum::App;
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[component]
struct Catalog;
```

Create the builder, discover linked registrations, then add the values and plugins this process owns:

```rust
let app = App::builder("catalog-api")
    .auto_discover()
    .with_component(Catalog)
    .build()
    .await?;
```

</Example>

`build()` is asynchronous because factories may perform asynchronous work. After it completes,
components have been validated and the selected protocol has built its runtime surface. Register
protocol-specific routes, services, and middleware through that protocol's builder extensions before
calling `build()`.

## Run the lifecycle

Use `run()` for a worker or another application whose components own all background work. It starts the application lifecycle and waits for Ctrl-C or `ShutdownHandle::shutdown()`.

<Example title="Run a background worker">

```rust
use upwell::axum::App;
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[component]
struct Indexer;
```

Build the worker's application, then let Upwell manage startup and shutdown hooks:

```rust
let app = App::builder("catalog-indexer")
    .auto_discover()
    .build()
    .await?;

app.run().await?;
```

</Example>

When the protocol accepts an explicit endpoint, call `app.serve(endpoint).await?` instead. The same lifecycle envelope starts hooks and reload triggers before serving, then runs shutdown cleanup when serving ends.

## Choose the process boundary

Use `app!` for the conventional application binary. It declares a named host and generates the process integration that direct builder code intentionally leaves to you.

<Example title="Let the host own the process">

```rust
use upwell::axum::Axum;
use upwell::prelude::*;
```

Declare the host instead of calling `.build().await` yourself:

```rust
app! {
    pub app CatalogApplication {
        name: "catalog-api",
        protocol: Axum,
    }
}
```

</Example>

`app!` and `App::<P>::builder` are complementary: choose the macro for generated startup, CLI, lifecycle, and Cargo Upwell tooling support; choose the builder for embedding or manually owned process behavior. See [the `app!` guide](app-macro) for a host-focused workflow and the <SymbolRef path="upwell_macros/app">canonical `app!` reference</SymbolRef> for every macro option.
